of the pitch marks is also kept for the models: the notation writes the arrows as placed (↑↓ a rise-fall, ↓↑ a fall-rise; before, always ↑↓), and the delivery names the tune's shape ("rise-fall on never"), not a meaning, so the context decides what it does (a final rise is a question, a continuation or a check; the fall-rise's uses: Ward & Hirschberg 1985, Language 61:747). Check: tests/ui.test.mjs R31 (the order in the notation, the delivery and the chat; the stress mark, the drawn-out vowel and the feeling's name set; the look styled and the stress mark hidden in the lighter look), RL1–RL4.
+**The voice in numbers (2026-11-10 (114); `voice.ts`).** A mark's name ("louder", "whispered") is an observation. The quantity is what a listener's ear receives. `soundOf(marks)` gives each word's sound against the speaker's own unmarked speech; the chat draws these quantities, and a word's hover text names them ("louder (about 6 dB above your normal voice)").
