+
+Rules: faster with slower leaves the speaker's own rate; louder with softer adds the two levels; a whispered word has the whisper's level whatever else it carries. Loudness in ratio: each 10 dB doubles it (the sone scale, Stevens 1936; Hänsler & Schmidt 2008, p. 299), so louder sounds 1.5 times as loud, softer 0.71 times, a whisper 0.16 times. Tempo is a ratio of the speaker's own rate because listeners tell two tempos apart from about 5 % (Quené 2007, J Phonetics 35:353). The layers follow the source–filter account of speech (Fant 1960, Acoustic Theory of Speech Production [not re-read in this pass]): breath and larynx set loudness, pitch and phonation; the tract above the larynx sets the formants; timing lies over both. Every mark above is a source or a timing quantity, so none needs a model of the tract. Open: the speaker's own baseline from the body (pitch in Hz from sex and age, the tract's length from height: Fitch & Giedd 1999, JASA 106:1511, 129 people aged 2–25); formants, which need the line's sounds (a pronouncing dictionary) first; a feeling setting these quantities itself (spoken anger, fear and happiness measured higher, louder and faster than sadness and tenderness: Quinto, Thompson & Keating 2013, Front Psychol 4:184, Table 1); the same table read by `effortOf` and the hearing rules. Check: tests/ui.test.mjs R32 (the levels equal `acoustics.ts`'s; the rules above), RM1, RM2.
+
+**Multiple distinctions (2026-11-03 (113), redrawn 2026-11-10 (114); `style.css` body.rules-distinct, `ui.ts` drawVoice and lookVars, `index.ts` applyLook, the setting "Multiple distinctions", on by default).** In the lighter look several marks share one visual channel, so a word's marks cover each other instead of multiplying (Garner 1974, The Processing of Information and Structure). The setting gives each part of the voice a place of its own around the word. The first drawing (113) was reported worse than the lighter look: the pitch stroke the most salient thing on the page, a blur worse than the old one, sizing errors. Measured in Chromium on 13 specimen lines (`history/review-tests/browser/voice-look/measure.mjs`): 11 lines changed their line height (by 1 to 9.75 px of 23), one word lay 2.2 px over the comma after it, 17 words were hollow and 10 blurred. The redrawing holds 13 of 13 lines at the story's line height, with no overlap, no blur and no hollow letter, in five fonts at 760 and 400 px.
+
+| Part of the voice | Place | Drawn as | Size, from the sound |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| melody (pitch) | above the word | one thin stroke in the word's own colour at 62 % opacity, left to right as the word is said, in the order given | 0.05 em per semitone: 6 semitones, 0.3 em |
+| loudness | in the letters | weight, size and opacity together | loudness ratio r = 2^(dB ÷ 10): weight 500 r (300–900), size r^0.27 (0.82–1.12), opacity r^0.37 (0.78 at least) |
+| phonation | under the run of words | a wavy rule (creaky), a row of dots (breathy), a broken rule (whispered) | none: a kind |
+| time | along the line | gaps and letters open or close; a faster word leans forward; a drawn-out vowel is written again per further beat ("Nooo"); a pause is a dot per syllable of silence | further time t = 1 ÷ rate − 1: gaps 0.3 em × t (−0.03 em at least), letters 0.08 em × t, lean 50° × t |
+| register | the letters' shape | a falsetto word 0.8 times as wide, as tall as its neighbours | none: a kind [ASSUMED 0.8] |
+| stress | before the word | the IPA's ˈ | none |
+| the feeling | the message's edge, and its word | a 2 px rule and the word, in the feeling's colour | OKLCH: hue the kind, chroma the activation, lightness the valence |
+
+Rules of the drawing:
+- A quantity with an order (pitch, loudness, time) takes a channel readers rank (position, size, weight, lightness). A kind (phonation, register, the feeling) takes a channel readers name (a line's style, a shape, a hue): Bertin 1967, Semiology of Graphics; Cleveland & McGill 1984, JASA 79:531 [neither re-read in this pass].
+- One part of the voice, one place. Three parts take a second cue, each for a stated reason. Loudness: cues that vary together are told apart better than one alone (4.1 against about 2.7 bits for size, brightness and hue: Eriksen, as Miller 1956, Psychol Rev 63:81, reports). Faster: letters close up by little before they touch, and a half-size width step was recognized by 55 % of readers against 78 % for the full one (Bessemans et al. 2019, Visible Language 53.3), so a faster word also leans. The feeling: colour and word, since two to five pairs of the five colours fall under 40 CIELUV units for a red-green colour-blind reader.
+- Readers read these cues once told them: bold as louder 81 %, wider as longer 93 %, raised as higher 83 % of 118 children; untold, they ignored them (Bessemans et al. 2019). So each marked word names its marks and their quantities on hover.
+- A drawing changes no line height: a word with a stroke, a lean or a narrowing is a block of line-height 1 on the baseline; a word or added word of another size takes line-height 0. This also holds in the lighter look since (114): its added words (inhales, the feeling, laughter's "ha") and its softer and falsetto words grew a line by 1 to 5 px.
+- A drawing blurs and empties no letter. Reading slows when letters lose their edges or their spacing (25 % at twice the standard spacing: Yu, Cheung, Legge & Chung 2007, J Vis 7(2):2), so the gains above keep letter spacing within 0.053 em of the font's own. The gains and bounds are [ASSUMED] for legibility (`ui.ts`, beside `lookVars`).
+- The sizes are the sound's: `lookVars()` derives each from `voice.ts`, `index.ts` sets them on the page as `--rules-…`, and `style.css` holds the same values as fallbacks (R32 keeps them equal).
