+- The feeling's colours are OKLCH coordinates (Ottosson 2020), one decision per coordinate. Hue: the colour terms 4,598 people in 30 nations matched with each emotion (Jonauskaite et al. 2020, Psychol Sci 31:1245): red with anger and love, yellow with joy, grey and black with sadness, pink with love, black with fear; here sadness is a grey with a blue cast and fear a violet [ASSUMED: black does not show on a dark theme]. Chroma: 0.13–0.15 for anger, fear and happiness, 0.045–0.085 for sadness and tenderness; more saturated colours are rated more arousing (Wilms & Oberfeld 2018, Psychol Res 82:896). Lightness: 0.84 for happiness and tenderness, 0.74 for the others on a dark theme (0.54 and 0.46 on a light one); lighter colours are rated more pleasant (same study). On every shipped theme each colour reads at 4.5:1 or more, and any two are 45 CIELUV units apart or more (dark themes 58), over the 40 from which colours are told apart dependably (Carter & Carter 1982, Appl Opt 21:2936, as the R package Polychrome states it). For a red-green colour-blind reader (deuteranope and protanope after Viénot, Brettel & Mollon 1999, Color Res Appl 24:243 [the matrices recalled, not re-read]) two to five pairs fall under 40, the closest 7 units apart (sadness and tenderness, protanope, light theme), so the word carries the feeling (`history/review-tests/browser/voice-look/palette-css.mjs`).
